   "id": "math-0016",
   "bucket": "reasoning",
   "planned_path": "P5",
   "scion_answer": "We have an unfair coin that lands heads with probability $p = \\frac{2}{3}$ and tails with probability $q = \\frac{1}{3}$.\n\nWe want the probability of exactly 2 heads in 5 flips. This is a binomial distribution problem:\n\n$$P(X = 2) = \\binom{5}{2} \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^2 \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^3$$\n\n$$= 10 \\cdot \\frac{4}{9} \\cdot \\frac{1}{27}$$\n\n$$= 10 \\cdot \\frac{4}{216}$$\n\n$$= \\frac{40}{216}$$\n\n$$= \\frac{5}{27}$$\n\nThe probability of getting exactly 2 heads is $\\boxed{\\dfrac{5}{27}}$.",
